A||N||18||2xsess||ABS light on in Van||||1||
Z||000000||2xsess||05-10-2001||08:15 AM||||The ABS light in our 97 transport started coming on after about 5' of moving. It satys on staedy, not flashing. Anyone have any insight as to what I should look at? Could it be a wheel sensor since it doesn't do it until you move the van? <P>This is the only thing that the (*^(%$#@ Warranty doesn't cover!!!!<P>Thanks for any help!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Jeff<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||JohnnyK||05-10-2001||12:24 PM||||Probably not a fuse since it only does it once you start to more.I think that a wire from a wheel sensor must have broken inside.. Your going to have to use a multimeter and start checking them all out. I dont' think I have ever seen an actual sensor go, but usually the wire breaks inside the insultation somewhere from constant moving/vibration/bending. <p>[This message has been edited by JohnnyK (edited 05-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||2xsess||05-10-2001||12:30 PM||||JohnnyK, Are the sensors supposed to have voltage or am I supposed to check for continuity?<P>Will the ABS system do a check like the ECM for the engine?<P><BR>Thanks,<BR>Jeff<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||05-10-2001||12:36 PM||||my 2 cents, I HATE ABS !!!, I know your stuck with it when you have it though, my Lumina minivan has it too. I dont know about GM, but a friend with a Chrysler 5th Ave had trouble with his. It was not dealer serviceable and he had to replace entire rear axle assembly for $1700.00. everything except his wheels and tires.||||reg||1
Z||000004||2xsess||05-10-2001||01:22 PM||||I couldn't agree more Roger.<P>Thanks for the little anecdote, got me really scared now <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||JohnnyK||05-10-2001||01:36 PM||||I can't remember exactly, but continuity comes to mind. It's probably just a wire, or worst case.. a sensor.. don't worry man.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||william_thorniii||05-10-2001||02:29 PM||||My folks had a 95 Trans Sport.  It had a recurring problem with the ABS system.  The dealership would claim that the problem was the Master Cyl.  The problem would go away for a while then it would come back.  They finally got rid of the problem, when they traded it in.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000007||2xsess||05-10-2001||02:36 PM||||I don't know if they used the same braking system but the 95 Transport is a totally different body style than the 97 I believe.<BR>Ours looks like a Montana. <P>Oh how I'd love to trade it in  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||05-10-2001||03:19 PM||||my Lumina is a 94, same as Transport and Silouette. Their all clones with diff badging. When they changed to new body, I still think there mechanicly almost same as earlier models.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||05-10-2001||08:49 PM||||Functionally ABS wheel sensors and Crank Position Sensors are the same.<P>1. Goto nhtsa.gov and make sure there are no recalls. There have been a bunch for diferent vehicles w/ ABS problems. (Use the same links as in my cave article.)<P>2. Check you warrantee coverage. A 97 may still have this covered....<P>3. Pull each wheel and physically inspect the sensors. Follow the wires as far as you can.<P>Because of the way these sensors work.... You can check them for continuity but that doesn't mean they are good. It's a bit hard to explain in a sentance or 3...<P>Otherwise you are going to have to have the thing connected to a scanner and read out the codes. Keep in mind not all PCM scanners can read the ABS codes. You may have to go to a dealer for this. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||PBJ||05-10-2001||09:10 PM||||It is very likely a frt wheel speed sensor, which is part of the frt hub. You can check it by unpluging sensor at brake rotor area and finding resistance-even better would be to spin wheel by hand and measuring small voltage created.  One will be likely zero.  The hubs are not hard to change. Part is about $350-400 cdn.  Very comon-never seen a harness go bad on a "U" van.||||reg||1
Z||000011||2xsess||05-10-2001||09:25 PM||||Ogre, I tried the Recall site and I got this message<BR><B>Due to an error in the raw data for this search a number of records are missing. We apologize for any inconvenience this might have cause</B><P>I guess I'll try again later.<P>Thanks for the tips though, I'll try them tomorrow.<P>I love this place  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Thanks,<BR>Jeff<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||baldlobo||05-10-2001||09:56 PM||||check your brakes if they are ok; then unplug the battery.||||reg||1
Z||000013||2xsess||05-10-2001||10:17 PM||||If the rear brake shoes are worn down would that cause it? I know the fronts are good, I just put new rotors and pads on there.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000014||theogre||05-10-2001||11:30 PM||||Unplugging battery will usually not clear errors from OBD2 computers. Many of these systems use flash ram. they will store codes w/o power.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||JohnnyK||05-11-2001||12:50 AM||||Brake shoes should have nothing to do with it. That would be the metallic scraping sound.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||fierospeeder||05-13-2001||03:22 AM||||get the popular mechanics CD from kmart, they should have the specs on how to diagnose the system.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000017||2xsess||05-13-2001||04:14 AM||||Ok, here's where it gets really odd. I followed my iwfe to the tranny place that rebuilt it 2 weeks ago to have them check it out to see if they F%#@ed anything up. Well, they had our van all day and called at 4:30 to tell us that they couldn't find out what it was but the tranny's working perfect(uh huh) So we go get it. ABS light comes on with in 15 feet of leaving their shop. So I'm like well let's take it to this other shop. we go there and the guy was really cool and honest. He said that he doesn't have the equipment to troubleshoot it and would end up taking it to the dealer. Wife and I decide to take it to the dealer on Monday. As we were taking it over to drop it off the first time I noticed that the only brake light working was the 3rd brake light. So, we get home incident free and safe. I decide to pull the taillights out to see WTF is wrong with them. with wife in the van pressing on the pedal, I take the right taillight off and wow, the brake light goes on. I wiggle the crap out of the wires and it doesn't even flicker. I go over to the left light and bang on it and it comes on  too. Now this is really ticking me off. There's got to be something wrong with the stupid connectors at the lights. So anyways, now that I have the brake lights working, well when they want to but always at least one working, there has been no ABS idiot light. We even put like 150 miles on it today running around locally.<P>Oh and that perfect working tranny....it makes some funky heat shield rattling around noise when it's under load. that's the only noise I can compare it too. All I know is that something's not right up there and I'm leaving next Sunday, my wife's pregnant and this stupid *%^*)_)(*&^%$#@ van is acting like a total POS!! My little 15 year old $1500 Fiero has given me less problems. oh, and the warranty runs out in less that 1000 miles.<P>~~~deep breath~~~~ ok, I feel better now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">...we;;. not really but ya know <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Jeff<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||hugh||05-13-2001||01:53 PM||||That rattling noise could be a loose torque converter.Since it is still under warranty,don't waitto go back and break their stones.||||reg||1
